; uart_tx.loom
; 8N1 UART transmitter on OUT0. Bytes come from the host through this
; thread's INQ (host writes FIFO space, thread 0). One tick = one bit period.
;
; This is the M1 acceptance program (docs/PLAN.md): it must produce the same
; byte stream the M0 hard-wired transmitter produced, decoded by the same
; cocotb model (test L3-UART-TX).
;
; Assembler syntax (tools/loomasm, M1): one instruction per line, labels end
; with ':', ';' starts a comment, '.thread', '.pins', '.csr' are directives.
; Pin names come from isa/isa.yaml (OUT0 = index 16).

        .thread 0
        .pins   TX = OUT0
        .csr    TICK_INT,  434          ; 50 MHz / 115200 = 434.03 clocks per bit
        .csr    TICK_FRAC, 0

start:  SETP    TX, 1                   ; line idles high

byte:   POP     r0                      ; block until the host pushes a byte
        SETD    0                       ; re-anchor: deadline = now (POP took an unknown time)
        WAITD   1                       ; align to the next tick edge (0 to 1 bit), so that
                                        ; the start bit below is a whole bit long
        SETP    TX, 0                   ; start bit
        WAITD   1                       ; hold exactly one bit period
        LDI     r1, 8

bit:    SHRI    r0, 1                   ; C = the bit to send (LSB first)
        BC      one
        SETP    TX, 0
        JMP     next
one:    SETP    TX, 1
next:   WAITD   1                       ; both paths land here; the deadline, not the
                                        ; path length, sets the bit boundary
        DJNZ    r1, bit

        SETP    TX, 1                   ; stop bit
        WAITD   1
        JMP     byte

; Timing notes for the deadline checker: the longest path between two WAITD
; instructions is the data-bit loop taken the long way round, DJNZ, SHRI, BC,
; SETP, JMP, WAITD = 6 slots = 24 clocks, far under the 434-clock tick, so the
; schedule is met with 410 clocks of margin. Each data-bit SETP executes the
; same 3 slots after its deadline on both paths, so the path a bit takes
; through the loop does not move its edge.
;
; What the edges actually look like: a thread gets an X cycle only every 4
; clocks (docs/SEMANTICS.md section 2, "Slot grid"), so a WAITD completes at
; the first X cycle at or after its deadline and each edge lands 0 to 3 clocks
; late. That lateness never accumulates, because WAITD advances TD from the
; previous deadline and not from "now". At TICK_INT 434 consecutive bit edges
; therefore alternate 432 and 436 clocks apart while the 10-bit frame spacing
; is exactly 4340 clocks, which is what a receiver samples against. A tick
; period that is a multiple of 4 clocks makes the lateness constant and the
; edges exactly periodic; 432 and 436 are both within 0.5 per cent of 115200
; baud, well inside what 8N1 tolerates.
;
; Why the extra WAITD after SETD (docs/spec-questions/firmware.md item 8): after
; an idle POP, SETD 0 lands anywhere inside a tick, and a start bit driven
; straight away would last only until the next tick edge, 0 to 1 bit. Waiting
; for that edge first makes it a whole bit: on the golden model the start bit
; measures 444 clocks at TICK_INT 434 whatever the idle time was (the start
; edge fires one slot after its deadline and the data edges four, hence the
; 2 per cent; SETP ... D at M2 makes every edge exact). The price is one idle bit time
; between back-to-back bytes (11 bit times per frame); firmware/uart_tx_fifo.loom
; ticks faster than the bit rate and keeps full throughput. uart_hello.loom
; anchors once, right after its CSRW TICK_INT clears the tick accumulator, so
; only its first start bit is short, by the few slots before its SETD (about
; 2 per cent of a bit), which no receiver notices.
;
; Bit-engine variant (M2): replace the bit loop with
;       LDSR r0 ; CSRW CNT, r1 (8) ; loop: SHO ; WAITD 1 ; DJNZ r1, loop
; which is 2 slots per bit and lets the same code run at 1 Mbaud and above.
